# Avoiding Common React State Mistakes

State bugs often come from treating a state variable like a normal variable. React only re-renders when you give a setter a *new* value, and the setter does not change the variable on the very next line. Keeping those rules in mind helps you avoid the mistakes below.

Common mistakes include:

- putting a prop or an unchanging constant into state when it never changes,
- mutating arrays or objects in state instead of creating new ones,
- expecting state to update immediately on the next line,
- storing a value in state that you could compute from other state,
- using a plain variable where you actually need a state variable.

## Do Not Put Props or Constants in State

State is for values that change over time in response to the user. If a value never changes, you do not need state for it. A common mistake is copying a prop into state:

```jsx
function Greeting({ name }) {
  const [displayName, setDisplayName] = React.useState(name);
  return <p>Hello, {displayName}</p>;
}
```

Nothing here ever calls `setDisplayName`, so `displayName` can never change. The state variable adds no value and can even go stale if the `name` prop changes later. Reference the prop directly instead:

```jsx
function Greeting({ name }) {
  return <p>Hello, {name}</p>;
}
```

The same rule applies to a plain constant: if a value is fixed, use a regular `const`, not `React.useState`.

## Do Not Mutate Arrays in State

React decides whether to re-render by checking whether the value you passed to the setter is a *new* object. If you change the existing array in place, React sees the same array reference and skips the re-render.

Wrong:

```jsx
items.push('New item');
setItems(items);
```

`items.push('New item')` does add the item, but it mutates the existing array, so React still thinks `items` is unchanged and does not re-render. Create a new array instead:

```jsx
setItems((items) => [...items, 'New item']);
```

The `...items` spread copies the previous values into a brand-new array, so React can tell the value changed and re-renders the list.

## Do Not Store Derived State

If you can compute a value from other state, do not keep a second state variable for it. Storing that derived value means you have to remember to update it everywhere, and the two values can drift out of sync.

Storing the count separately is unnecessary:

```jsx
const [items, setItems] = React.useState(['A', 'B']);
const [numItems, setNumItems] = React.useState(2);

function addItem() {
  setItems((current) => [...current, 'C']);
  setNumItems((n) => n + 1);
}
```

The number of items is always `items.length`, so compute it during render instead of storing it:

```jsx
const [items, setItems] = React.useState(['A', 'B']);
const numItems = items.length;

function addItem() {
  setItems((current) => [...current, 'C']);
}
```

Now there is only one source of truth, and `numItems` can never fall out of sync with `items`.

## Use State When a Value Needs to Trigger a Re-render

The opposite mistake is using a plain variable when you actually need state. A regular variable can change, but React has no way to know it changed, so the screen never updates:

```jsx
function Counter() {
  let clickCount = 0;
  return <button onClick={() => clickCount++}>{clickCount}</button>;
}
```

A value the user changes that should appear on screen belongs in `React.useState`, and you update it by calling its setter.

## Example

```{react}
function TodoList() {
  const [items, setItems] = React.useState(['Read']);
  const count = items.length;

  function addItem() {
    setItems((currentItems) => [...currentItems, 'Practice']);
  }

  return (
    <section>
      <button onClick={addItem}>Add</button>
      <p>{items.join(', ')}</p>
      <p>Count: {count}</p>
    </section>
  );
}

renderReact(<TodoList />);
```

- `items` is the single source of truth for the list.
- `count` is *derived* from `items` during render, so it never drifts out of sync.
- `addItem` creates a new array with the spread syntax, so React re-renders.

## Exercises

1. Which state update avoids mutation?

   ```{mcq}
   Which state update avoids mutation?
   correct: Correct. The spread syntax creates a new array with the added item.
   wrong: Think about whether the old array is changed directly.
   [x] `setItems((items) => [...items, 'New'])`
   [ ] `items.push('New')`
   [ ] `items[0] = 'New'`
   [ ] `items.length = 0`
   ```

2. Fix the array update so clicking adds `Practice`.

   ```{react}
   function TodoList() {
     const [items, setItems] = React.useState(['Read']);

     function addItem() {
       items.push('Practice');
       setItems(items);
     }

     return (
       <section>
         <button onClick={addItem}>Add</button>
         <p>{items.join(', ')}</p>
       </section>
     );
   }

   renderReact(<TodoList />);
   ```
   ```+solution
   function TodoList() {
     const [items, setItems] = React.useState(['Read']);

     function addItem() {
       setItems((currentItems) => [...currentItems, 'Practice']);
     }

     return (
       <section>
         <button onClick={addItem}>Add</button>
         <p>{items.join(', ')}</p>
       </section>
     );
   }

   renderReact(<TodoList />);
   ```
   ```+test
   // React commits its render asynchronously, so the assertions below run
   // on the next tick. Asserting immediately would find an empty container.
   setTimeout(function () {
     wrap(source.includes('push(')).should('equal', false).run('Do not mutate the array with push', 'Avoided array mutation');
     assert('button').click().run('Click Add', 'Clicked Add');
     // The state update re-renders asynchronously too, so give it a tick.
     setTimeout(function () {
       assert('p').should('have.text', 'Read, Practice').run('Add Practice to the displayed list', 'Updated the list');
     }, 50);
   }, 50);
   ```

3. This component stores the item count in a second state variable, and the count is stuck at `1`. Remove the `count` state and derive it from `items.length` instead so the count updates when you add items.

   ```{react}
   function Cart() {
     const [items, setItems] = React.useState(['Apple']);
     const [count, setCount] = React.useState(1);

     function addItem() {
       setItems((current) => [...current, 'Apple']);
     }

     return (
       <section>
         <button onClick={addItem}>Add</button>
         <p>Count: {count}</p>
       </section>
     );
   }

   renderReact(<Cart />);
   ```
   ```+solution
   function Cart() {
     const [items, setItems] = React.useState(['Apple']);
     const count = items.length;

     function addItem() {
       setItems((current) => [...current, 'Apple']);
     }

     return (
       <section>
         <button onClick={addItem}>Add</button>
         <p>Count: {count}</p>
       </section>
     );
   }

   renderReact(<Cart />);
   ```
   ```+test
   // React commits its render asynchronously, so the assertions below run
   // on the next tick. Asserting immediately would find an empty container.
   setTimeout(function () {
     wrap(source.includes('setCount')).should('equal', false).run('Remove the separate count state and its setter', 'Removed the derived state variable');
     assert('button').click().run('Click Add', 'Clicked Add');
     assert('button').click().run('Click Add again', 'Clicked Add again');
     // The state update re-renders asynchronously too, so give it a tick.
     setTimeout(function () {
       assert('p').should('have.text', 'Count: 3').run('The count should equal the number of items', 'Count stays in sync with items');
     }, 50);
   }, 50);
   ```

4. A `Greeting` component receives a `name` prop and never calls the setter. Why should you reference `name` directly instead of copying it into state?

   ```{mcq}
   A Greeting component copies its name prop into state with React.useState(name) but never calls the setter. Why is the state variable unnecessary here?
   correct: Correct. The value never changes, so state adds nothing and can even go stale if the prop changes.
   wrong: Consider whether the value ever changes and what state is actually for.
   [x] The value never changes, so state adds nothing and can drift out of sync with the prop.
   [ ] Props cannot be read inside a component body.
   [ ] React.useState is not allowed to receive a prop as its initial value.
   [ ] State variables make JSX invalid.
   ```

